module gunbc.scm.log

// THE LOG VERB: which commits this envelope carries.
//
// It answers exactly that one question. It is not a formatting engine: the result is a sequence of
// typed rows, and turning rows into text is a rendering concern that belongs with the process-output
// boundary. Nothing here chooses a separator, a column order or a width, because a decision that can
// only be justified by how the output would LOOK cannot be derived from the envelope, and this
// module is built entirely from what the envelope carries.
//
// WHAT THE ENVELOPE CARRIES: a RepositoryCommit has its minted reference, root, message and explicit
// ancestry. So a log row can name the commit, name the corpus manifest it points at, and carry the
// message. (The root was a PROGRAM when this was written and is now a `CorpusManifestObjectRef` -- an
// authored snapshot, not a semantic node. Found by external review, along with the two below.)
// TWO FIELDS A USER WOULD EXPECT ARE ABSENT FROM THE MODEL, and their absence is reported
// here rather than synthesised:
//
//   AUTHOR      -- no commit in this layer records who made it.
//   TIMESTAMP   -- no commit records when.
// Each would have to be invented to appear in a row, and inventing them is the fabricated-plausible-
// output failure DESIGN section 5 forbids at a user-facing boundary.

import std.types { Bool, List, String }
import gunbc.scm.object_store {
  CorpusManifestAbsent,
  CorpusManifestFound,
  CorpusManifestIsAuthoredSource,
  CorpusManifestIsSemanticNode,
  CorpusManifestObjectRef,
  ObjectStore,
  find_corpus_manifest_record,
}
import gunbc.scm.repository_envelope { RepositoryEnvelope, RepositoryCommit }

// WHETHER THIS COMMIT'S PROGRAM IS ACTUALLY HELD, which is decidable here and therefore reported.
// The envelope's own encoder refuses on RepositoryCommitRootNotInStore, so "this commit names a root
// the store does not contain" is a state the model already knows how to recognise. Listing such a
// commit without saying so would present a row that cannot be checked out as though it were
// ordinary -- the same reasoning that gives gunbc.scm.status its CheckedOutCommitMissing arm, and
// the same refusal it is derived from.
// THREE STANDINGS, NOT TWO, BECAUSE THE STORE ANSWERS TWO DIFFERENT NEGATIVES.
//
// A commit root must denote a CORPUS MANIFEST: it is the snapshot `checkout` materializes. The store
// also holds authored-source objects and semantic nodes, so a locator can be PRESENT and still be the
// wrong kind of thing for a root. (This paragraph said "semantic node" from when a root WAS a program;
// the arm it describes is `LoggedCommitManifestHeld`, which already had the corrected name.) An
// earlier revision asked `store_contains`, which is deliberately kind-agnostic -- it answers about the
// LOCATOR -- and so reported a file sitting under a commit's root locator as `LoggedCommitRootHeld`: a
// log row claiming a checkoutable root where checkout refuses. The two
// negatives have different remedies (fetch the missing object vs. repair a wrong-kind reference), so
// they are separate arms rather than one Bool.
//
// A CORPUS MANIFEST IS A THIRD SUCH THING, and it arrives by the same route: the store holds one, a
// locator can be present and be it, and a log row claiming a checkoutable root over one would be the
// same false report this type was split to prevent. It is named rather than folded into the authored
// -source arm because a log is read by a person deciding what to repair, and "there is a file here"
// sends them somewhere different from "there is a corpus manifest here".
// THE STANDING IS ABOUT THE SNAPSHOT DESIGNATION ALONE, AND ITS SUCCESS ARM IS DELIBERATELY NARROW.
// `LoggedCommitManifestHeld` says exactly one thing: this store holds a corpus manifest at the root
// this commit names. It does NOT say the corpus is recoverable, and it must never be rendered as
// though it did -- a manifest's entries name authored sources this store may not hold, and that is a
// SEPARATE observation over the entries which this function does not perform. Advertising held-ness
// as "checkoutable" would be a well-formed answer about a larger question than the one asked, which
// is the defect this module's neighbours keep paying for.
//
// The wrong-kind arms swapped with the root's kind rather than thinning: a semantic node occupying
// the root locator is now the wrong kind, and an authored source still is. That swap is exactly why
// the prose above needed correcting rather than the arms -- the CODE tracked the manifest recut and
// the sentences describing it did not.
type LoggedCommitStanding
  = LoggedCommitManifestHeld
  | LoggedCommitRootMissingFromStore
  | LoggedCommitRootIsAuthoredSource
  | LoggedCommitRootIsSemanticNode

// The row exposes the commit's own opaque identity, not a value derived from its list position or
// root. Consumers may hand it back as a reference; they must not inspect its ordinal for ordering or
// recency, because the allocator watermark is uniqueness state rather than chronology.
type CommitLogRow {
  reference: RepositoryCommitRef
  root: CorpusManifestObjectRef
  message: String
  standing: LoggedCommitStanding
}

// AN EMPTY HISTORY IS ITS OWN STATE, NOT AN EMPTY LIST.
//
// A consumer handed `[]` has to decide what it means, and the two available readings have different
// remedies: "this repository has no commits yet" is the ordinary state of a fresh repository
// (repository_envelope's header: `init` produces no commits and no root, and neither is an error),
// while "the log produced nothing" is the shape a failed or unperformed observation takes. DESIGN
// names that collapse the empty-observation narrow and rules it strictly worse than the widen it
// mirrors, because a widen is merely expensive and a narrow is silently uncovered.
//
// Making the distinction structural means no consumer can accidentally treat one as the other:
// there is no empty CommitLogRows value to confuse with NoCommitsYet, because the fresh-repository
// case has its own constructor and never produces a row list at all.
type CommitLog
  = NoCommitsYet
  | CommitLogRows { rows: List<CommitLogRow> }

type CommitLogAcc {
  rows: List<CommitLogRow>
}

fn logged_commit_standing(store: ObjectStore, root: CorpusManifestObjectRef) -> LoggedCommitStanding {
  match find_corpus_manifest_record(store: store, identity: root.locator) {
    CorpusManifestFound(_) => LoggedCommitManifestHeld
    CorpusManifestAbsent { identity: _ } => LoggedCommitRootMissingFromStore
    CorpusManifestIsAuthoredSource { identity: _ } => LoggedCommitRootIsAuthoredSource
    CorpusManifestIsSemanticNode { identity: _ } => LoggedCommitRootIsSemanticNode
  }
}

fn commit_log_step(store: ObjectStore, acc: CommitLogAcc, commit: RepositoryCommit) -> CommitLogAcc {
  CommitLogAcc {
    rows: concat(
      acc.rows,
      [
        CommitLogRow {
          reference: commit.reference,
          root: commit.root,
          message: commit.message,
          standing: logged_commit_standing(store: store, root: commit.root),
        },
      ],
    ),
  }
}

// Rows preserve the authored commit-list order, but references do not derive from that order.
//
// WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CLAIM IS RECENCY, and the reason is a finding rather than a
// preference: NOTHING IN THE MODEL ESTABLISHES THAT A LATER INDEX IS A NEWER COMMIT. No commit
// carries a timestamp, and position is an artefact of how a list was assembled rather than of when
// anything happened. Presenting these rows newest-first would manufacture chronology out of position,
// which is the same fabrication as inventing the timestamp itself, one step less obvious.
//
// THE REASON GIVEN FOR THIS USED TO BE FALSE, and correcting it does NOT license the ordering it
// refused. It said there is no commit-authoring verb and every `RepositoryCommit` is constructed by a
// witness. That stopped being true in this cut: `gunbc.scm.write_spine` `commit_staged` and
// `gunbc.scm.squash_merge` `squash_merge` are both producers, and each appends a commit whose parent
// it knows. Found by external review.
//
// BUT TWO REAL PRODUCERS DO NOT MAKE THE LIST CHRONOLOGICAL, and this is exactly where a stale premise
// invites an over-correction. `commits` is also populated by `gunbc.scm.repository_envelope`
// `decode_repository`, which reconstructs a commit list from a document in whatever order that
// document happens to carry -- an order this function cannot see and the decoder does not claim to
// preserve. So the honest statement is narrower than either the old claim or its negation: SOME
// commits now have a producer that defines what appending meant, and the list as a whole still has no
// ordering basis, because a property that holds of some members of a population is not a property of
// the population.
//
// (AN EARLIER WRITING OF THIS PARAGRAPH -- MINE, IN THE COMMIT THAT REPLACED THE STALE PREMISE -- also
// named "importing a history" as a second such route. THERE IS NO IMPORT ROUTE. No producer of that
// name exists anywhere in `gunbc.scm`, and I invented it while correcting an annotation for asserting
// a capability the model does not have. Caught by external review asking whether the route I cited had
// a producer. The argument does not need it: one non-appending route is enough to defeat a claim that
// every member was appended in order.)
//
// This is still not a tiebreak waiting to be chosen -- a tiebreak presumes a partial ordering basis,
// and here the basis is absent. What would establish one is a per-commit fact every route must supply,
// not a verb that some routes happen to use.
fn repository_log(envelope: RepositoryEnvelope) -> CommitLog {
  if count(envelope.commits) == 0 {
    NoCommitsYet
  } else {
    match fold(
      envelope.commits,
      init: CommitLogAcc { rows: [] },
      f: fn(acc, c) { commit_log_step(store: envelope.store, acc: acc, commit: c) },
    ) {
      CommitLogAcc { rows: rows } => CommitLogRows { rows: rows }
    }
  }
}

// Whether every logged commit names a corpus manifest this repository actually holds -- held-ness of
// the SNAPSHOT DESIGNATION, which is not recoverability of its entries; `LoggedCommitManifestHeld`
// says the manifest object is present, and whether every source it names resolves is the separate
// observation this function does not perform. A consumer deciding
// whether the image is usable wants this without walking the rows itself, and it is a FACT rather
// than a rendering, so it belongs beside the log rather than in whatever eventually prints it.
fn commit_log_is_fully_held(log: CommitLog) -> Bool {
  match log {
    NoCommitsYet => true
    CommitLogRows { rows: rows } =>
      fold(rows, init: true, f: fn(held, r) {
        if held {
          match r.standing {
            LoggedCommitManifestHeld => true
            LoggedCommitRootMissingFromStore => false
            LoggedCommitRootIsAuthoredSource => false
            LoggedCommitRootIsSemanticNode => false
          }
        } else {
          false
        }
      })
  }
}
